onical location convention | Requires: the repository pattern for .agents canonical directories and .claude compatibility symlinks | Why: establishes the exact relocation result.\\n3. Rebase relocation inventory | Requires: every tracked file and location reference in the rebase ecosystem | Why: moving an incomplete ecosystem or leaving stale paths breaks discovery.\\n4. Receiving-review relocation inventory | Requires: every tracked file, symlink, executable mode, and location reference in that ecosystem | Why: preserves an already-conforming layout.\\n5. Agent-writing standard | Requires: writing-for-agents and Skill Mechanics criteria | Why: the requested audit needs a concrete, repository-available standard.\\n6. Forge-specific surface inventory | Requires: current GitHub-only instructions, flags, API operations, models, tests, and fallback behavior | Why: GitLab parity cannot omit behavior that GitHub users retain.\\n7. Forge-neutral workflow contract | Requires: equivalent fetch, validate, reply, resolve, batch, watch, blocker, and repository-detection behaviors where each forge exposes them | Why: equal ease requires one workflow rather than separate manual procedures.\\n8. GitLab transport capability | Requires: supported GitLab CLI and API operations for host detection, pagination, merge-request discussions, replies, and resolution | Why: implementation must rest on available platform operations.\\n9. GitHub transport preservation | Requires: authenticated REST or GraphQL, pagination, and hostname selection remain supported | Why: the task forbids losing existing GitHub capabilities.\\n10. Evaluation target | Requires: the exact ~/repos/svs-gitlab repository, its GitLab remote, and a usable glab executable | Why: supplies a real self-managed GitLab discovery surface.\\n11. Verification coverage | Requires: existing receiving-review tests, new provider-specific tests, instruction validation, and read-only GitLab smoke checks | Why: parity and non-regression 